"""`Console`: what a task shows in the terminal. On by default.

It covers two things: the lines Aspect prints around a task, and Bazel's
console on every `ctx.bazel.build` / `ctx.bazel.test` that leaves stdout and
stderr to the CLI (`aspect build`, `aspect test`, `aspect lint`, ...).

`--console=false` (or `--console:enabled=false`, or
`ctx.features[Console].enabled = False`) sets
both aside: Aspect's lines take the `full` layout, whatever the four layout
args say; Bazel's stderr is left inherited, as `bazel` itself shows it, unless
another feature adds stages of its own; and the tip rules are off, built-in
and yours.

On the command line the args are kebab-case: `--console:recap-max=20`,
`--console:template-preset=compact`.

Aspect's lines
--------------

The terminal is a status surface like the CI ones and takes the same four
args. `template_preset` picks a built-in layout and `templates` overrides
single slots on top of it for every task; `task_presets` and `task_templates`
do the same for one task, keyed by task kind ("lint") or command path
("worktree add"):

    load("@aspect//feature/console.axl", "Console")

    def config(ctx):
        ctx.features[Console].args.template_preset = "compact"
        ctx.features[Console].args.templates = {"header": "== {{ label }}"}
        ctx.features[Console].args.task_presets = {"worktree add": "silent"}
        ctx.features[Console].args.task_templates = {
            "lint": {"verdict": "{{ glyph }} lint {{ verb }}{{ timing }}"},
        }

The default, `full`, reads the same on a terminal and in a plain CI log:

    ▸ Running build task

    ▸ Setup
    ▹ Running setup

    ▸ Build
    ▹ Spawning bazel build
    INFO: Bazel 9.3.3
    ...
    INFO: Build completed successfully, 40 total actions

    ✓ Passed build task in 1m 12s · Setup 0.4s · Build 1m 11s

On Buildkite each phase opens a log section, its heading and first update on
the section's line. Under every preset a phase that runs Bazel opens expanded
(`+++`), so its errors are never in a collapsed group, and so does the verdict,
which opens the last:

    --- :aspect: Running build task
    --- Setup · Running setup
    +++ Build · Spawning bazel build
    INFO: Bazel 9.3.3
    ▹ Uploading results
    +++ ✓ Passed build task in 1m 12s · Setup 0.4s · Build 1m 11s

Its parts take the theme's `task`, `task.label`, `phase`, `phase.title`,
`phase.update`, `phase.text` and `verdict.*` roles (see `docs/themes.md`).
`emoji` keeps the lines Aspect printed before, with an emoji on each and
Buildkite emoji shortcodes.

A task with its own preset starts from it alone, without `templates`;
`task_templates` beat everything else, a command path's over its kind's. A
`template_preset` typed on the command line applies to every task, over
`task_presets`.

The runtime does the rendering: it reads these args after `config.axl` runs
and before the header prints. Bazel's own output, warnings and errors, and
stdout are never templated, and nor is the end of Bazel's console (the
failure summary and what was hidden): it prints under every preset, `compact`
and `agent` included. A tip's first
line is the `tip` slot, `TIP[id] suggestion: title`; `emoji` and `silent`
add the severity's emoji (`TIP[id] 💡 suggestion: title`), and a template
that renders blank hides the tip:

    ctx.features[Console].args.templates = {
        "tip": "{{ highlight_style }}{{ severity }}{{ highlight_reset }} [{{ id }}] {{ title }}",
    }

Under a coding agent (any of the environment variables `lib/agent.axl` reads,
such as `CLAUDECODE`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` or `AI_AGENT`) the default is `agent`
rather than `full`. Setting `template_preset` on the command line or in any
`config.axl`, even to `full`, turns that off.

The preset also decides whether detail lines print: the `INFO:` asides a
reader can do without, which `detail` in `lib/environment.axl` prints (timings,
filter counts, uploaded-artifact notes). `full` and `emoji` show them,
`compact`, `agent` and `silent` hide them, and `verbose` forces them on or off
whatever the preset. `verbose = "debug"` also prints the `DEBUG:` lines of
`debug` in `lib/environment.axl`, the internals a bug report needs. The level
comes from the first of: `--console:verbose` on the command line, the
environment (`ASPECT_DEBUG=1` means `debug`, else `ASPECT_VERBOSE`, which takes
the same values), `verbose` set in a `config.axl`, the preset.

Levels, quietest first: `debug`, `detail`, `info`, `warn`, `error`. `info`
lines, which carry a task's result or why it did nothing (`No files to
format`), always print, as do `WARNING:` and `ERROR:` lines, a task's own exit
message (`TaskConclusion(message = ...)`, `ctx.std.process.exit`), the
`announce_bazel_*` disclosures, which have their own flags, and the
`aspect worktree` report.

Bazel's console
---------------

It puts `default_stages` from `@aspect//bazel/console.axl` on
`BazelTrait.output_stages`:
- paths are made relative;
- the first warning of each kind is shown and the rest are counted at the end;
- every failure Bazel's build events report is listed at the end, under
  `── Failures (N) ──`, an entry each, in groups: the targets' (a root whose
  analysis or loading failed, a missing source), the failed actions, the
  tests. An entry is its heading (`✗ //pkg:t  Genrule · exit 1 ·
  pkg/BUILD.bazel:3:8`), then, for the first five, an excerpt of what the
  tool said behind a `│` (a test's from its failed attempt's `test.log`),
  its log's path and the targets it blocked. Past `--console:recap-max`
  entries (10) the heading says `first 10 of 14` and the rest are counted.
  Bazel's `ERROR:` lines that tie to no entry exactly are listed under
  `── Other errors from Bazel ──`, on a passing build too. Every build
  then ends with one line of counts (`193 built, 1 failed · 47 tests
  passed, 2 failed · Bazel's raw log: <path>`) and `All of it: aspect
  history show <id>`. Under the Dashboard the summary goes to the
  scrollback only, when the screen is left;
- a run of identical lines, an action's output included, shows as its first
  and a count, but never Bazel's progress or status lines;
- a tip is given for failures we recognize: the tip rules of
  `@aspect//bazel/console_tips.axl` and `TipsTrait.rules`.

Nothing is dropped by default.

Bazel's console as it wrote it (its stderr, and its stdout when both go to
one file) is kept in a raw log, `console.log` in the Bazel invocation's folder
in the task's history, which the end of the console names (a link on a
terminal), with how many messages were hidden and, for each kind collapsed, a
file beside the log holding all of them. They are kept and pruned with the
rest of the history.

On a terminal, the URLs in Bazel's console and the failure summary, and the
paths that name a file that exists (`src/a.cc:3:4` links to `src/a.cc` at
line 3), are links to open it in an editor too, unless a coding agent is reading it, it is a CI
job's, or `TERM` is `dumb`. What a path's link opens is `file_hyperlink_format`:
your own choice is `"editor": "cursor"` in `~/.aspect/config.json` (or
`ASPECT_FILE_HYPERLINK_FORMAT=cursor` in your shell's profile), which beats a
team default of `"editor"` in the repository's `.aspect/config.json` (an
editor's name, `auto` or `none` there, never a URL template, so a cloned
repository cannot make a click open a scheme of its choosing), which beats
`ctx.features[Console].args.file_hyperlink_format = "cursor"` in any
`config.axl`: the repository's, as below, or your own `~/.aspect/config.axl`.
By default, your editor at the line, the one whose terminal this is, else the
one `$VISUAL` or `$EDITOR` starts, else one installed; none on a remote
machine (SSH, a dev container, Codespaces). In Terminal.app,
which cannot show such a link, its URL is written beside the path, muted
and underlined where colour is shown, and `https://` URLs are underlined.

    load("@aspect//feature/console.axl", "Console")

    def config(ctx: ConfigContext):
        # Collapse your build's own noise, and drop what nobody reads.
        ctx.features[Console].args.collapse = [r"^WARNING: .* is deprecated"]
        ctx.features[Console].args.drop = [r"^DEBUG: .*rules_python"]
        # Leave out a default stage by name.
        ctx.features[Console].args.disable = ["relativize_paths"]
        # Repeat up to 20 errors at the end, rather than 10.
        ctx.features[Console].args.recap_max = 20

A tip is turned off by `--tips:silence=<id>` (repeat it for several; it
joins a `config.axl`'s Tips `silence` rather than replacing it), a
`config.axl`'s `silence`, or `{"tips": {"silence": ["<id>"]}}` in a
`config.json`: yours in `~/.aspect/config.json` or the repository's
`.aspect/config.json`; every list counts. The `config.axl` files are the
exception: they run in turn and each assigns `silence`, so your
`~/.aspect/config.axl`'s list replaces the repository's rather than
joining it.

Tips of your own are rules next to the built-in ones;
`--tips:surfaces=<id>=<surface>,...` moves one:

    load("@aspect//tips.axl", "TIP_SHOW_FIRST", "TIP_WARNING", "TipsTrait", "on_console_line", "tip_rule")

    ctx.traits[TipsTrait].rules.append(tip_rule(
        id = "cache-token",
        when = on_console_line(r"PERMISSION_DENIED.*remote cache"),
        show = TIP_SHOW_FIRST,
        severity = TIP_WARNING,
        title = "Your remote cache token expired",
        body = "Run `acme login`.",
    ))

A feature of your own adds a stage by appending a `bazel.output.<stage>(...)`
to `ctx.traits[BazelTrait].output_stages`; stages run in a fixed order of
kinds, so it does not matter which feature appends first.

`--console:profile` picks how the console is shown. `auto`, the default, is
`agent` when a coding agent's environment variables are set and `human`
otherwise. Bazel is always captured with its curses UI, which tells the
stages where each action's output ends; a terminal shows it, and anything
else gets plain lines with Bazel's status line every few seconds. The agent
profile shows plain lines on a terminal too, and no status line, since an
agent reads the console rather than watching it. Your own `--curses` (in an
rc or `--bazel-flag`) decides what is shown, under either profile, unless
you turned progress off (`--noshow_progress`), which shows plain lines
without the status line.

Only messages Bazel colours can be told from the output of the actions it
runs, so Bazel is always asked for colour too, and what is shown keeps it as
your own `--color` says, else as Aspect's own lines do: on a terminal or a CI
log that renders it, as `NO_COLOR`, `CLICOLOR_FORCE`, `CLICOLOR` and `TERM`
allow.

A stage setting that is not valid (an unknown stage name, a pattern that does
not compile) is left out with a warning rather than failing the task: this
feature runs for every task, most of which never start Bazel. A tip rule id
used twice is the exception, and fails: it is always a mistake in a config.
A template that does not compile, unlike these, fails the run before the
task starts.
"""

load("@aspect//bazel/console.axl", "PROFILES", "RECAP_MAX", "STAGE_NAMES", "console_tip_rules", "default_stages", "emit_tips", "tip_stages")
load("@aspect//bazel.axl", "BazelTrait")
load("@aspect//tips.axl", "TipsTrait")
load("../private/lib/agent.axl", "under_agent")
load("../private/lib/bazel_results.axl", "bb_clientd_root")
load("../private/lib/environment.axl", "warn")
load("../private/lib/failure_records.axl", "failure_records")
load("../private/lib/failures.axl", "failures")
load("../private/lib/task_template_args.axl", "TASK_PRESET_PRECEDENCE", "task_template_args")
load("../private/lib/term_text.axl", "term_text")

def _console_impl(ctx: FeatureContext):
    if ctx.args.recap_max < 1:
        ctx.std.process.exit(1, "--console:recap-max=%d: the most failures the summary lists must be 1 or more" % ctx.args.recap_max)
    profile = ctx.args.profile
    if profile == "auto":
        profile = "agent" if under_agent(ctx.std) else "human"
    got = default_stages(
        profile = profile,
        disable = ctx.args.disable,
        collapse = ctx.args.collapse,
        drop = ctx.args.drop,
        recap_max = ctx.args.recap_max,
        root = lambda: ctx.std.env.bazel_root_dir(),
        cwd = lambda: ctx.std.env.current_dir(),
        summary = True,
    )
    for problem in got.problems:
        warn(ctx.std, "--console: " + problem)
    trait = ctx.traits[BazelTrait]
    trait.output_stages.extend(got.stages)

    # What the failure summary ties Bazel's lines from, whichever of the
    # console's stages are on.
    trait.output_stages.append(bazel.output.collect_errors())

    # The tip rules are read as each build starts and ends, not now: a feature
    # whose implementation runs after this one may still append some.
    tips = ctx.traits[TipsTrait]
    warned = {}

    def _tip_stages(task_ctx):
        matched = tip_stages(console_tip_rules(tips.rules))
        for problem in matched.problems:
            if problem not in warned:
                warned[problem] = True
                warn(task_ctx.std, "--console: " + problem)
        return matched.stages

    trait.output_start.append(_tip_stages)

    # Each build's aggregates count once in the task's tips, however many
    # tips a rule emits from them.
    builds = [0]

    def _emit_tips(task_ctx, build):
        builds[0] += 1
        emit_tips(task_ctx, build, console_tip_rules(tips.rules), batch = str(builds[0]))

    trait.output_end.append(_emit_tips)

    # The failure summary at the end of each build's console: every failure
    # once with what Bazel said of it, the counts, Bazel's raw log and where
    # the rest is. Not under the Dashboard (`output_display` `none`), which
    # shows failures in its own tab and puts the summary in the scrollback.
    summary = [None]

    def _fresh(task_ctx):
        return failure_records.new_state(task_ctx.std.fs.try_read_tail, bb_clientd_root(task_ctx.std.env), task_ctx.std.re.compile(term_text.ANSI))

    def _collect(task_ctx, event):
        if event.kind == "build_started" or summary[0] == None:
            summary[0] = _fresh(task_ctx)
        failure_records.collect_event(summary[0], event)

    # The summary lists as many failures as the recap would repeat errors,
    # with or without the Dashboard.
    trait.failure_summary_max = ctx.args.recap_max

    def _summarize(task_ctx, build):
        if trait.output_display == "none":
            return
        state = summary[0] or _fresh(task_ctx)
        state["build"] = build
        state["collected_errors"] = build.output_collected_errors()
        state["cancelling"] = task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled
        err = task_ctx.std.io.stderr
        size = task_ctx.std.term.size() if err.is_tty else None
        for line in failures.failure_summary(state, task_ctx.task.id, err.is_tty, build.output_log or "", task_ctx.std.io.linked, task_ctx.std.excerpt, size[0] if size else 100, build.exit_code, max_entries = trait.failure_summary_max):
            err.write(line + "\n")
        summary[0] = None

    trait.build_event.append(_collect)

    # Ahead of the tips the build's end emits, so the summary follows
    # Bazel's own end.
    trait.output_end.insert(0, _summarize)
    if got.display != "auto" and trait.output_display == "auto":
        trait.output_display = got.display
    if not got.progress:
        trait.output_progress = False

Console = feature(
    display_name = "Console",
    summary = "Choose the lines Aspect prints around a task, and make Bazel's console easier to read: relative paths, collapsed warnings and repeated lines, recapped errors, tips for failures it recognizes.",
    implementation = _console_impl,
    args = {
        # No `values`: the runtime checks the name, so a removed preset's error
        # says what replaced it, on the command line and in config.axl alike.
        "template_preset": args.string(
            default = "full",
            description = "Built-in terminal layout: 'full' (the banner, the header, each phase's " +
                          "heading and its updates, and the verdict with its phase breakdown, " +
                          "without emojis; default), 'emoji' (the lines Aspect printed " +
                          "before, with emojis and Buildkite emoji shortcodes), 'compact' (the verdict alone, " +
                          "on one line with no phase breakdown), 'agent' (compact without " +
                          "emojis; the default under a coding agent unless this is set on the " +
                          "command line or in config.axl), or 'silent' (nothing; the exit " +
                          "code, the task's own output and Bazel's console carry the result). 'compact', " +
                          "'agent' and 'silent' also hide detail lines; see `verbose`. On Buildkite the " +
                          "header, phase and verdict lines, and sections a task opens itself, " +
                          "start log sections, so they print under every preset; a phase that " +
                          "runs Bazel and the verdict open expanded (+++). " + TASK_PRESET_PRECEDENCE,
        ),
        "templates": args.custom(
            dict,
            default = {},
            description = "Jinja2 overrides on top of `template_preset`, keyed by slot: \"banner\" " +
                          "(`line`, `version`, `debug_build`, `docs_url`), \"header\" (`label`), " +
                          "\"phase\" (`text`, `title`, `emoji`, `continued`: an update to the phase " +
                          "already shown) and \"progress\" (`text`, `title`, `emoji`), \"verdict\" " +
                          "(`verdict`, `cancelled`, `glyph`, `shortcode`, `verb`, `label`, `exit_code`, `exit`, " +
                          "`elapsed`, `elapsed_ms`, `timing`, `conclusion`, `breakdown`, " +
                          "`plain_breakdown`, `timings`, `phases`; `timing` and the breakdowns follow " +
                          "--task:timing-summary), \"section\" (`icon`, `title`), the title of " +
                          "a Buildkite section a task opens itself, such as the Workflows runner " +
                          "metadata, and \"tip\" (`id`, `severity`, `emoji`, `title`, `tip_style`, " +
                          "`id_style`), a tip's first line, its body printed under it; a blank " +
                          "render hides the tip. Every slot also gets `buildkite` and `task` (`kind`, `group`, " +
                          "`name`, `path`); all but \"section\", and a \"progress\" update that " +
                          "names no phase, get `highlight_style` and `highlight_reset`, the ANSI " +
                          "codes around the line's highlight (empty when color is off). Every slot gets " +
                          "the escape of each of the theme's `task`, `task.label`, `phase`, `phase.title`, " +
                          "`phase.update`, `phase.text` and `verdict.*` roles as `<role>_style`, a `.` read as " +
                          "`_` (`phase_title_style`), and `reset`, which closes them. They apply to every task that renders with " +
                          "`template_preset` (not one with its own `task_presets` entry); one task's own go in " +
                          "`task_templates`. An empty template prints nothing, except on Buildkite, " +
                          "where a line that opens a log section takes the preset's text (or " +
                          "`emoji`'s, or `full`'s for a preset without emojis). A template that " +
                          "fails to render falls back to the preset's template for that slot.",
        ),
        "verbose": args.string(
            default = "auto",
            values = ["auto", "true", "false", "debug"],
            bare = "true",
            description = "Whether detail lines print: the INFO asides a reader can do without, " +
                          "such as timings and filter counts. 'auto' (default) leaves it to the " +
                          "task's preset: shown under 'full' and 'emoji', hidden under 'compact', " +
                          "'agent' and 'silent'. 'true' (or a bare --console:verbose) and 'false' " +
                          "force them on or off, and 'debug' also prints DEBUG lines; config.axl " +
                          "may also assign True or False. ASPECT_VERBOSE takes the same values and " +
                          "beats config.axl but not the command line; ASPECT_DEBUG=1 means " +
                          "'debug'. Other INFO lines, and WARNING and ERROR lines, always print.",
        ),
        "file_hyperlink_format": args.string(
            default = "auto",
            description = "What a link to a file opens, on a terminal, as ripgrep's --hyperlink-format: " +
                          "an editor's scheme at the line after the path (`vscode`, `vscode-insiders`, " +
                          "`vscodium`, `cursor`, `zed`, `idea`, `macvim`, `textmate`), `none`, or a URL " +
                          "template over {path}, {line}, {column} and {host}; never a file:// one, which " +
                          "has the system open the file and may run it. `auto` (the default) is `none` " +
                          "on a remote machine (SSH, a dev container, Codespaces), else the editor whose " +
                          "terminal this is (VS Code, VS Code Insiders, VSCodium, Cursor), else " +
                          "the one $VISUAL or $EDITOR starts, else the first installed of cursor, code, " +
                          "code-insiders, zed, codium, idea, mvim and mate (its command on PATH, or on macOS " +
                          "its app in /Applications or ~/Applications), else `none`. ASPECT_FILE_HYPERLINK_FORMAT and " +
                          "the top-level \"editor\" key of the user's ~/.aspect/config.json and of the repository's " +
                          ".aspect/config.json take the same values; the command line beats " +
                          "ASPECT_FILE_HYPERLINK_FORMAT, which beats the user's \"editor\", which beats the " +
                          "repository's (an editor's name, auto or none only, never a URL template), which beats any config.axl (the repository's or ~/.aspect/config.axl), " +
                          "and these are what config.axl's own lines follow. An invalid \"editor\" is a warning " +
                          "naming its file and is ignored, so the next decides. A directory, a binary file, and anything under /bin, /sbin, /usr/bin and " +
                          "the system's other directories never link. In Terminal.app, " +
                          "which shows no such link, the link's URL is written after the path and its line, " +
                          "muted and underlined where colour is shown, to open by Cmd+double-click or " +
                          "Control+click, but in a progress row Bazel redraws. URLs are links " +
                          "whatever it says.",
        ),
        "profile": args.string(
            default = "auto",
            values = ["auto"] + PROFILES,
            description = "How Bazel's console is shown: `human`; `agent`, for a coding agent reading the console, which shows plain lines without Bazel's progress rather than its live UI; or `auto` (the default), `agent` when a coding agent's environment variables are set. Your own `--curses` decides under either, unless `--noshow_progress` asks for plain lines.",
        ),
        "disable": args.string_list(
            description = "A default console stage to leave out: " + ", ".join(STAGE_NAMES) + ". Repeat for several.",
        ),
        "recap_max": args.int(
            default = RECAP_MAX,
            description = "How many failures the end-of-build summary lists, and of Bazel's other errors, 1 or more; the rest are counted (`aspect history show` has all).",
        ),
        "collapse": args.string_list(
            description = "A regex; Bazel messages whose first line it matches collapse into the first of them, whatever their severity. Never matches action or test output, nor the `INFO: From …` header above it. Repeat for several.",
        ),
        "drop": args.string_list(
            description = "A regex; Bazel messages whose first line it matches are hidden, with the indented lines under them. Never matches action or test output, nor the `INFO: From …` header above it. Repeat for several.",
        ),
    } | task_template_args("silent", "verdict"),
)
